WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.933
Seen from space,

2
00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:03.211
the mouths of the Ganga and the

3
00:00:03.211 --> 00:00:05.184
Brahmaputra look like the branches of a

4
00:00:05.184 --> 00:00:06.601
tree.

5
00:00:06.601 --> 00:00:09.188
This is the largest delta on Earth.

6
00:00:09.588 --> 00:00:11.260
A river carries silt.

7
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.479
When it reaches the sea,

8
00:00:12.479 --> 00:00:15.021
it slows down and drops that load.

9
00:00:15.021 --> 00:00:16.647
The silt piles up,

10
00:00:16.647 --> 00:00:18.133
blocks the way,

11
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:20.780
and the river splits into smaller channels,

12
00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.380
called distributaries.

13
00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:25.056
Over centuries the land grows outward in

14
00:00:25.056 --> 00:00:27.076
a fan, shaped like the Greek letter

15
00:00:27.076 --> 00:00:27.970
delta.

16
00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.860
That's where the name comes from.

17
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.570
Most big rivers of peninsular India flow

18
00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:35.032
east and build deltas.

19
00:00:35.032 --> 00:00:37.133
But the Narmada and the Tapi flow

20
00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:39.385
west, through rift valleys,

21
00:00:39.385 --> 00:00:41.659
and end in estuaries instead.

22
00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.279
They carry less silt,

23
00:00:43.279 --> 00:00:44.765
they run through hard rock,

24
00:00:44.765 --> 00:00:46.240
and the tides wash out what they

25
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.143
bring.

26
00:00:47.543 --> 00:00:49.192
For GS One:

27
00:00:49.192 --> 00:00:50.341
a river slows,

28
00:00:50.341 --> 00:00:51.235
drops its silt,

29
00:00:51.235 --> 00:00:52.640
and splits.

30
00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.997
East-flowing rivers build deltas;

31
00:00:54.997 --> 00:00:57.781
the Narmada and the Tapi make estuaries.

